1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Rodrigo Muñoz de Guzmán''' or '''Rodrigo Núñez de Guzmán''' (died ca. 1186), considered the common ancestor of the noble house of Guzmán,[1] was a Castilian magnate and tenente of Roa and of the village of Guzmán in Burgos, fro
2 CONC m which this lineage took its name.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Life
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing is known about the parentage or ancestry of Rodrigo Muñoz de Guzmán. The onomastic customs of that time dictate that, given his patronymic Muñoz, he must have been the son of a Munio (Muño) or Nuño.[a] 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a patron of several monasteries, including the Monastery of San Cosme and San Damián in Covarrubias and of the Monastery of San Cristóbal de Ibeas situated in San Millán de Juarros which had been founded by Álvaro Díaz de O
2 CONC ca and his wife Teresa Ordóñez, the maternal grandparents of his wife Mayor. Rodrigo and Mayor were buried there. On 20 February 1151, Gutierre Fernández de Castro with his wife Toda, and Rodrigo and his wife Mayor, Toda's sister,
2 CONC  donated several properties that the sisters had inherited from their maternal grandparents, to the abbot of the monastery.[1][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Rodrigo Muñoz probably died shortly after 29 January 1186, the date when he last appears in medieval charters.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Marriage and issue
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000225465841832&size=large|center|Atrium and tower of the Colegiata de San Cosme y San Damián de Covarrubias}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He married before 1150 '''Mayor Díaz''', daughter of Diego Sánchez, who died in the Battle of Uclés, and of Enderquina Álvarez, daughter of Álvaro Díaz de Oca and his wife Teresa Ordóñez.[1][2] 
2 CONT
2 CONT At least nine children were born of this marriage:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Álvaro Rodríguez de Guzmán (died in 1187),[4][5] governed, as tenant-in-chief, Mansilla, a toponymic which he adopted as his cognomen and as such he appears confirming several charters as Álvaro Rodríguez de Mansilla.[6] He also
2 CONC  governed La Pernía and Liébana.[b] [c][7][8] Álvaro married Sancha Rodríguez de Castro, daughter of Rodrigo Fernández de Castro, the Bald and Eylo Álvarez with whom he had Fernando and Eylo Álvarez,[9] as well as Elvira and Toda 
2 CONC Álvarez de Guzmán, the latter married to Álvaro Rodríguez Girón, son of Rodrigo Gutiérrez Girón.[10]
2 CONT # Fernando Rodríguez de Guzmán, who, according to genealogist Luis de Salazar y Castro, was the husband of Juana de Aza and father of Saint Dominic, although there is no documentary proof whatsoever sustaining this filiation.[6][2
2 CONC ][d]
2 CONT # Nuño or Munio Rodríguez de Guzmán, who probably died young, shortly after August 1151 when he last appears confirming a donation made by King Sancho III of Castile.[12]
2 CONT # Pedro Rodríguez de Guzmán (died on 18 July 1195 at the Battle of Alarcos).[13] Appointed mayordomo mayor of King Alfonso VIII in April 1194.[14] Around May 1174 he married Mahalda, as confirmed in the donation made by King Alfon
2 CONC so VIII in casamento (as a wedding gift).[15][16][e] The couple had several children, including Nuño Pérez de Guzmán who was the head of the house of Guzmán,[15] and Guillén Pérez de Guzmán, the father of Mayor Guillén de Guzmán, 
2 CONC mistress of King Alfonso X of Castile, the parents of Beatrice of Castile, queen consort of Portugal due to her marriage to King Afonso.
2 CONT # Rodrigo Rodríguez de Guzmán, mentioned by his brother Álvaro in 1167.
2 CONT # Urraca Rodríguez de Guzmán (died after 1189), who married Pedro Rodríguez de Castro.[19][20][21] Both appear together in 1189 when they donated Villasila and Villamelendro to the Order of Santiago, confirming as ego Petrus Roder
2 CONC ici de Castro cum uxore mea Urraca Roderici (I, Pedro Rodríguez de Castro with my wife Urraca Rodríguez)
2 CONT # Sancha Rodríguez de Guzmán,[21] mentioned by her brother Álvaro in 1167.
2 CONT # María de Guzmán,[21] was the first wife of Rodrigo Gutiérrez Girón.[7]
2 CONT # Teresa Rodríguez de Guzmán,[21] also mentioned by her brother Álvaro in 1167.
2 CONT # 
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT *a. According to the Spanish genealogist Luis de Salazar y Castro, Rodrigo was the son of Álvar Núñez de Guzmán and Elvira de Manzanedo, "ignoring the most basic usage of patronymics during the 10th, 11th, and 12th centuries".[2] 
2 CONC The historian Gonzalo Martínez Diez dismisses this filiation as pure fiction and concludes that there is no documentary evidence enabling the identification of the Munio (Muño) or Nuño who would have been the father of Rodrigo.[2]
2 CONT *b. He appears frequently with his siblings. In 1170 appears with his brothers Pedro and Fernando confirming the treaty of Zaragoza: "... Albaro rodriz de massiella, Petrus rodriz et ferrandus rodriz eius fratres".[6]
2 CONT *c. In another document dated 1167, he mentions his brothers and sisters: Pero, Fernando, Rodrigo, Urraca, Sancha and Teresa.
2 CONT * d. "We know nothing about the marriage or descendants of Fernando Rodríguez de Guzmán. Because of this lack of documentation and the fact that there was no record of any Félix de Guzmán, the supposed father of Saint Dominic, Sal
2 CONC azar y Castro proposed the hypothesis, totally unfounded, that this Fernando was the father of Saint Dominic of Caleruega (...) We have checked several branches of the Aza and Guzmán lineages without having found Juana de Aza (sup
2 CONC posed mother of the saint) or any Félix of Guzmán" (loose translation)[11]
2 CONT * e. In old genealogical treatises, Pedro's wife is said to be a member of the Manzanedo family although this filiation is not confirmed in medieval charters. Jaime de Salazar y Acha suggests that she could have been an illegitima
2 CONC te daughter of William VIII of Montpellier and, therefore, half-sister of Maria of Montpellier, the mother of King James I of Aragon Sánchez de Mora proposes that Mahalda could have been the daughter of Manrique Perez de Lara, alt
2 CONC hough she does not appear with her supposed parents or siblings in any document.[17][18]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Guzman
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the well-known treatistist Don Francisco Piferrer, the origins of the Guzmán lineage are somewhat obscure, which increases when the surname to be studied is the older and nobler.
2 CONT
2 CONT This sometimes leads to a lack of solidity, as the researcher is required to resort to mere conjectures. All this comes up, according to what was exposed by the aforementioned treaty, since in this case it occurs in the five main 
2 CONC Castilian plots, Lara, Haro, Castro, Guzmán and Villamayor, whose primitive parents are not easy to designate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Adhering to the lineage of the Guzmán, we find that some pretend to descend from the Dukes of Brittany, by means of a knight called Goodman or Gudman, which means, in English, "good man", who was a rich man in the reign of Don Fer
2 CONC nando I, and founded the tower of Guzmán, near the place of Roa, in the bishopric of Osma.
2 CONT
2 CONT Others indicate that this is not the case, but that Guzmán comes from Flavio Gundemaro, twenty-first Gothic king of Spain, who died in Toledo, in 612, and others, raise the origin of this lineage to even more remote times.
2 CONT
2 CONT But leaving aside all of the above, which, as Piferrer rightly indicates, are still mere conjectures, we will have to refer to what is historically proven: Don Rui Núñez, a rich man who lived in the twelfth century, was the first 
2 CONC to take the surname Guzmán, for being the Lord of the aforementioned Torre de Guzmán, near Roa.
2 CONT
2 CONT And although some of his ancestors already had the Lordship of this tower, they had not taken the name, because we already say that until the end of the 12th century people did not start talking about him. Don Rui married Doña Goz
2 CONC o González de Lara and had as children Alvar, or Alvaro, who did not leave succession, Don Pedro, through whom the manly of this house continued, and Don Félix, who married Doña Juana de Aza, and from whose marriage Santo Domingo 
2 CONC de Guzmán was born.
2 CONT
2 CONT WEAPONS:
2 CONT
2 CONT Flanked shield: Head and azure tip, a gold boiler jaquelada of gules, with six sirpe heads on each handle; silver flanks, five saber ermines in aspa or sotuer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Www.redheraldica.com
2 CONT
2 CONT Señor de Guzmán, Rich Man of Castile (Charlín)
